      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/alestopetersius-leopoldianus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Alestopetersius leopoldianus originates from Lac Mai Ndombe (formerly: Lake Leopold) and the middle Congo.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/09-characoids-tetra-relationship/alestopetersius-leopoldianus-2/",
      "html": "<p>Depending on the incidence of light the animal looks completely different. With the naked eye one almost always sees a dark, broad longitudinal band. This is also the only significant coloration feature mentioned in the first scientific description from 1899.</p><p>The deep blue dorsum is striking. Depending on the angle of light, the reflective colors can be so intense on the body that they even cover the longitudinal band, or make it almost invisible.</p><p>In the aquarium all these colors can be seen at once, because the fish swims and so the incidence of light also changes permanently.</p>"
    }
  ],
